From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 14:20:57 +0200
Subject: net: lantiq: xrx500-cbm: terminate the switch receive ring on the CPU

The LAN switch writes received frames into a descriptor ring in memory
over a receive DMA channel. With a peripheral-to-peripheral relay in
place, that ring is never read by software. The relay hands every frame
to the second switch instance's transmit channel, the frame re-enters
the buffer manager on the far side, and a load spreader distributes
completions over four interrupt lines. That buys receive spreading in
hardware at the price of the whole dequeue-manager and load-spreader
layer, and it puts the frame through two switch macros and three DMA
controllers before the processor sees it.

Consume the ring directly instead. The conduit allocates a ring of its own,
resets the channel and points it there, and polls it from a NAPI context with
generic receive offload. Nothing of the dequeue manager, the load spreader or
the second switch instance is involved, and the frame crosses the datapath
once.

The frame is copied into a socket buffer rather than handed up on the ring
buffer. The switch header is eight bytes and the engine addresses memory in
eight-byte units, so the Ethernet header can be aligned for the engine or
aligned for the network layer but not for both, and an unaligned network
header on this architecture is a fault rather than a slowdown. A parameter
asks the engine for a byte offset inside the buffer, which is what a
copy-free path would need. The buffer is sized for the largest offset the
field can hold and for a frame check sequence, because the descriptor is
armed with what is left of the buffer behind the offset: sizing it for the
frame alone would shorten the frame that fits instead of growing the buffer,
and at the largest offset even a minimum-size buffer would report every frame
as malformed.

The source port arrives in the switch header rather than in the descriptor,
whose endpoint field is zero on this path, so the header stays in the socket
buffer until it is consumed and the ports are counted by it. A switch tagger
takes that over once the ports are network devices of their own. The frame
takes its protocol from eth_type_trans(), which returns it rather than
storing it; a socket buffer that reaches the stack without one matches no
protocol handler, and the core then accounts the drop against the device.
The received header is laid out as in datapath/datapath_api_gswip30.h,
lines 353 to 386, of the AVM GPL release.

A descriptor the ring rejects is counted on the interface as well as in the
driver's own statistics, so a ring full of nonsense does not look like an
idle interface to ordinary tools, and the first few of them are dumped at
debug level, bounded by a fixed count, because neither count says which
field was wrong. A rejected descriptor is rearmed like any other, and the
transfer of ownership back to a device that does not snoop the caches sits
next to the rearm so that every path reaches it. A failure to unmask the
channel after a poll is reported rather than discarded: a receive path that
goes silent with nothing to read is the failure mode hardest to tell from a
dead one.

Receive ownership is off by default, so that loading the driver leaves
the ring to whatever already consumes it. Taking the ring over stops the
relay channel first. That stop is the only thing standing between a ring
this driver has taken over and a relay a previous boot may have left
running.

The comments state the constraints. Their sources in the AVM GPL release
are these files:

- drivers/dma/intel/hdma.h, which carries one descriptor structure per
  strapping
- cqm/grx500/lantiq_cbm_api.h line 72, the receive channel wired as a
  switch target
- the receive ring seeding in drivers/dma/intel/hdma.c, the order in
  which a receive descriptor is handed over

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

